    <h1>Umbra</h1>
    <p class="umbra-tagline">A local-first AI studio for running, orchestrating, and auditing large language models on hardware you own. This page describes what it's meant to do. It isn't finished, there's nothing to download, and the design may still change.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="umbra-section">
    <h2>What it's meant to be</h2>
    <p>The design target is a desktop GUI and terminal TUI for local models: loading and switching models, running multi-step inference, inspecting prompt/run records, and reviewing runs after the fact. On-device inference and no product telemetry are goals, not released guarantees. Most of it is still being built.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="umbra-section">
    <h2>Target hardware: NVIDIA DGX Spark</h2>
    <p>Umbra is being developed on an <strong>NVIDIA DGX Spark</strong> — Grace Blackwell compute with 128&nbsp;GB of unified memory — and that's the hardware it targets. The memory budget is what the design leans on: larger models resident in memory, longer contexts, and room to inspect a run while it generates. CUDA first. I haven't tested it anywhere else.</p>
    <div class="umbra-pills">
      <span>NVIDIA DGX Spark</span>
      <span>CUDA-first</span>
      <span>128 GB unified memory</span>
      <span>GUI + TUI</span>
      <span>offline target</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="umbra-section">
    <h2>Status</h2>
    <p>Early development. Not released, not installable, and no date to give you. Some of what's described here works on my machine; some of it is still a plan. I'd rather say that than imply otherwise.</p>
    <p>The intent is for it to be free for individuals, with paid licensing for organizations that want team deployment and support. That isn't settled.</p>
    <p>If you want to follow along, watch <a href="https://github.com/penumbraforge" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/penumbraforge</a> or write to <a href="mailto:shadoe@penumbraforge.com">shadoe@penumbraforge.com</a>.</p>
